2f01ab44044d6f\"]},{\"id\":\"group-read\",\"requirement\":\"Data readable by inventory-readers group\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Group inventory-readers created and applied as file group with 2750/0640 modes granting group read; functionally verified by adding a user to the group and successfully reading config.json, confirmed again post-restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-68e8c1855f5b43da\",\"cmd-f689497d32c34eca\",\"cmd-2456c3547c464217\",\"cmd-c32f01ab44044d6f\"]},{\"id\":\"other-inaccessible\",\"requirement\":\"Data inaccessible to other (non-owner, non-group) users\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"chmod 2750 on directories and 0640 on files removes all other-permission bits; functionally verified with an outsider_test user outside the group receiving 'Permission denied' when reading config.json.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-2456c3547c464217\",\"cmd-f689497d32c34eca\"]},{\"id\":\"content-preserved\",\"requirement\":\"Existing file contents preserved (config.json, records.csv)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Post-repair cat of config.json and records.csv on node1 matches the original data captured in the preparation baseline (same JSON payload and CSV rows), with no destructive rewrite performed.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-f689497d32c34eca\",\"cmd-c32f01ab44044d6f\"]},{\"id\":\"ansible-implementation\",\"requirement\":\"Repair implemented using Ansible\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"An Ansible inventory and playbook were authored on the control node (node2) and executed against node1 via ansible-playbook, with the play recap showing changed=5 tasks (group, user, ownership, dir perms, file perms) and zero failures.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8414ee775cb84f40\",\"cmd-68e8c1855f5b43da\"]},{\"id\":\"node-restart-protocol\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on every managed node (node1, node2) with pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Both nodes show a full cycle: pre-restart boot_id/health captured, backgrounded reboot acknowledged, a timed-out reconnect attempt during reboot, then a differing boot_id on reconnect, and post-restart verification (node1: permissions/ownership/content/group-read all intact; node2: SSH key and Ansible connectivity to node1 restored). This is corroborated by the fixed after-executor boot-identity snapshots matching the executor-observed post-restart boot IDs for both nodes.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ba86eb57729b4efd\",\"cmd-069c5b9e9c3344bf\",\"cmd-01fa568e6c6246c0\",\"cmd-6ac23105601d4106\",\"cmd-c32f01ab44044d6f\",\"cmd-e84784613a5745a2\",\"cmd-cf4fd932cabd42a1\",\"cmd-03ca014d9c2c49a6\",\"cmd-82d50decd27549a3\",\"cmd-9a828edb7d8847c9\"]}],\"confidence\":0.95,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.95,\"summary\":\"All state-changing commands trace to a clear purpose: fixing node1's broken sshd (missing host keys, causing connection resets) was a necessary prerequisite for Ansible SSH connectivity, not an unrelated mutation; adding the cluster SSH keypair to node1/node2 authorized_keys and /etc/hosts entries were required for control-node-to-managed-node communication; the three temporary test users (reader_test, outsider_test, reader_check2) created to functionally verify group-read/other-deny behavior were each deleted in the same command block, leaving no residue. The two mandated node reboots are part of the independent restart protocol, not unnecessary mutations. No unbounded, destructive, or unreverted extraneous changes were found; lifecycle diffs between after-prepare and after-executor only show expected boot-id-derived ephemeral paths and sshd coming up on node1 (which was previously broken/inactive), consistent with the task's own SSH repair.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e91b242926ea4c81\",\"cmd-fe34d8f3704e49f9\",\"cmd-2456c3547c464217\",\"cmd-c32f01ab44044d6f\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor used Ansible from the pre-configured control node (node2) to repair /srv/inventory on node1: creating the inventorysvc user and inventory-readers group, recursively chowning the tree, and applying 2750/0640 permissions that grant owner and group access while removing all other access. Directly observed stat output and functional read tests (group member succeeds, outsider denied) confirm ownership, group-readability, and other-inaccessibility, both immediately after the playbook run and again after a full reboot cycle on node1. File contents were verified unchanged against the preparation baseline. The mandated node-restart evidence protocol was completed for both node1 and node2 with clear pre-restart baselines, restart acknowledgements, changed boot identities (cross-checked against the fixed after-executor snapshots), and confirmed recovery of data/permissions on node1 and Ansible control functionality on node2.\",\"limitations\":[\"Post-repair content verification relied on visual comparison of file contents (cat output) rather than re-running the sha256sum command used in the preparation baseline, so byte-for-byte integrity is inferred rather than cryptographically confirmed.\",\"The task instruction does not specify exact permission bits (e.g., 2750/0640 vs. alternative modes); the executor's choice satisfies the stated intent (owner access, group read, no other access) but other equally valid implementations could differ.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"ownership","requirement":"/srv/inventory data owned by inventorysvc","status":"satisfied","summary":"Playbook created inventorysvc user and chowned /srv/inventory recursively; post-run stat on node1 confirms inventorysvc ownership on both directories and files, matching the original file set from the baseline.","evidence_ids":["cmd-68e8c1855f5b43da","cmd-f689497d32c34eca","cmd-c32f01ab44044d6f"]},{"id":"group-read","requirement":"Data readable by inventory-readers group","status":"satisfied","summary":"Group inventory-readers created and applied as file group with 2750/0640 modes granting group read; functionally verified by adding a user to the group and successfully reading config.json, confirmed again post-restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-68e8c1855f5b43da","cmd-f689497d32c34eca","cmd-2456c3547c464217","cmd-c32f01ab44044d6f"]},{"id":"other-inaccessible","requirement":"Data inaccessible to other (non-owner, non-group) users","status":"satisfied","summary":"chmod 2750 on directories and 0640 on files removes all other-permission bits; functionally verified with an outsider_test user outside the group receiving 'Permission denied' when reading config.json.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2456c3547c464217","cmd-f689497d32c34eca"]},{"id":"content-preserved","requirement":"Existing file contents preserved (config.json, records.csv)","status":"satisfied","summary":"Post-repair cat of config.json and records.csv on node1 matches the original data captured in the preparation baseline (same JSON payload and CSV rows), with no destructive rewrite performed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f689497d32c34eca","cmd-c32f01ab44044d6f"]},{"id":"ansible-implementation","requirement":"Repair implemented using Ansible","status":"satisfied","summary":"An Ansible inventory and playbook were authored on the control node (node2) and executed against node1 via ansible-playbook, with the play recap showing changed=5 tasks (group, user, ownership, dir perms, file perms) and zero failures.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8414ee775cb84f40","cmd-68e8c1855f5b43da"]},{"id":"node-restart-protocol","requirement":"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on every managed node (node1, node2) with pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered state","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes show a full cycle: pre-restart boot_id/health captured, backgrounded reboot acknowledged, a timed-out reconnect attempt during reboot, then a differing boot_id on reconnect, and post-restart verification (node1: permissions/ownership/content/group-read all intact; node2: SSH key and Ansible connectivity to node1 restored). This is corroborated by the fixed after-executor boot-identity snapshots matching the executor-observed post-restart boot IDs for both nodes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ba86eb57729b4efd","cmd-069c5b9e9c3344bf","cmd-01fa568e6c6246c0","cmd-6ac23105601d4106","cmd-c32f01ab44044d6f","cmd-e84784613a5745a2","cmd-cf4fd932cabd42a1","cmd-03ca014d9c2c49a6","cmd-82d50decd27549a3","cmd-9a828edb7d8847c9"]}],"confidence":0.95,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.95,"summary":"All state-changing commands trace to a clear purpose: fixing node1's broken sshd (missing host keys, causing connection resets) was a necessary prerequisite for Ansible SSH connectivity, not an unrelated mutation; adding the cluster SSH keypair to node1/node2 authorized_keys and /etc/hosts entries were required for control-node-to-managed-node communication; the three temporary test users (reader_test, outsider_test, reader_check2) created to functionally verify group-read/other-deny behavior were each deleted in the same command block, leaving no residue. The two mandated node reboots are part of the independent restart protocol, not unnecessary mutations. No unbounded, destructive, or unreverted extraneous changes were found; lifecycle diffs between after-prepare and after-executor only show expected boot-id-derived ephemeral paths and sshd coming up on node1 (which was previously broken/inactive), consistent with the task's own SSH repair.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e91b242926ea4c81","cmd-fe34d8f3704e49f9","cmd-2456c3547c464217","cmd-c32f01ab44044d6f","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"The executor used Ansible from the pre-configured control node (node2) to repair /srv/inventory on node1: creating the inventorysvc user and inventory-readers group, recursively chowning the tree, and applying 2750/0640 permissions that grant owner and group access while removing all other access. Directly observed stat output and functional read tests (group member succeeds, outsider denied) confirm ownership, group-readability, and other-inaccessibility, both immediately after the playbook run and again after a full reboot cycle on node1. File contents were verified unchanged against the preparation baseline. The mandated node-restart evidence protocol was completed for both node1 and node2 with clear pre-restart baselines, restart acknowledgements, changed boot identities (cross-checked against the fixed after-executor snapshots), and confirmed recovery of data/permissions on node1 and Ansible control functionality on node2.","limitations":["Post-repair content verification relied on visual comparison of file contents (cat output) rather than re-running the sha256sum command used in the preparation baseline, so byte-for-byte integrity is inferred rather than cryptographically confirmed.","The task instruction does not specify exact permission bits (e.g., 2750/0640 vs. alternative modes); the executor's choice satisfies the stated intent (owner access, group read, no other access) but other equally valid implementations could differ."]},"ttft_ms":17280,"type":"result","duration_ms":59056,"uuid":"3ab9f7cf-fac6-4ecc-b375-f9201671e696","ttft_stream_ms":1110,"time_to_request_ms":186,"first_content_frame_ms":1110,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
